public boolean tryClaim(ParkingSpot spot) {
    if (spot.getStatus() == SpotStatus.EMPTY) {   // read
        spot.setStatus(SpotStatus.OCCUPIED);      // write
        return true;
    }
    return false;
}
Two threads run this method concurrently on the same `spot` object, which starts with `status = EMPTY`, with no synchronization at all. Both threads happen to reach the `if` check before either thread reaches the `setStatus` line. What is the realistic outcome?

A developer attempts to fix a race condition like this:
public boolean tryClaim(ParkingSpot spot) {
    Object lock = new Object();   // created fresh, every single call
    synchronized (lock) {
        if (spot.getStatus() == SpotStatus.EMPTY) {
            spot.setStatus(SpotStatus.OCCUPIED);
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
If two threads call `tryClaim(spot)` on the same `spot` object at nearly the same time, does this actually prevent both from succeeding?

public class SpotAllotter {
    private final Object lock = new Object();

    public boolean tryClaim(ParkingSpot spot) {
        synchronized (lock) {
            if (spot.getStatus() == SpotStatus.EMPTY) {
                spot.setStatus(SpotStatus.OCCUPIED);
                return true;
            }
            return false;
        }
    }
}
Two different threads call `tryClaim()`, but each thread is working with its own, separately-constructed `SpotAllotter` instance (`new SpotAllotter()` was called twice, instead of both threads sharing one instance). Does `lock` still protect against both threads claiming the same spot?

To reduce lock contention, a developer proposes locking per resource *category* instead of one global lock:
private final ConcurrentHashMap<VehicleType, Object> locksByVehicleType = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

private Object getLockFor(VehicleType type) {
    return locksByVehicleType.computeIfAbsent(type, t -> new Object());
}

public boolean tryClaim(ParkingSpot spot, VehicleType requestedType) {
    synchronized (getLockFor(requestedType)) {
        if (spot.getStatus() == SpotStatus.EMPTY) {
            spot.setStatus(SpotStatus.OCCUPIED);
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
Suppose a single physical spot supports multiple vehicle types — for example, one large spot supports both TRUCK and CAR. Thread 1 calls `tryClaim(thatSpot, VehicleType.CAR)` and Thread 2 calls `tryClaim(thatSpot, VehicleType.TRUCK)` on that exact same spot object, at nearly the same time. What bug does this locking strategy introduce?

Here is a corrected claiming method that locks on the spot itself and re-checks status inside the lock:
public boolean tryClaim(ParkingSpot spot) {
    synchronized (spot) {
        if (spot.getStatus() == SpotStatus.EMPTY) {
            spot.setStatus(SpotStatus.OCCUPIED);
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
`spot.status` starts as EMPTY. Two threads call `tryClaim(spot)` on this exact same `spot` object at nearly the same moment. What is guaranteed about the result?

