Given that the actual running code in this class still uses `getLockFor(requestedType)` (per-vehicle-type locking), is the teammate's belief — that no further work is needed — correct?

An engineer writes a service method:
@Transactional
public void updateEmail(Long userId, String newEmail) {
    User user = userRepository.findById(userId).orElseThrow();
    user.setEmail(newEmail);
}
A code reviewer flags that userRepository.save(user) was never called and claims the update will not be persisted to PostgreSQL. Is the code reviewer's claim correct, and how does JPA handle this mutation?

A funds transfer service executes:
@Transactional
public void transferMoney(Long fromId, Long toId, BigDecimal amount) throws InsufficientFundsException {
    debitAccount(fromId, amount);
    if (checkFraudRules(fromId)) {
        throw new InsufficientFundsException("Account flagged");
    }
    creditAccount(toId, amount);
}
InsufficientFundsException extends java.lang.Exception (a checked exception). When the exception is thrown, the developer discovers that debitAccount was committed to the database anyway, leaving customer balances corrupted. Why did @Transactional fail to roll back the transaction, and how must it be fixed?

In a distributed microservices platform, when a ticket booking is confirmed, the BookingService must commit the booking record to PostgreSQL and publish a BookingConfirmedEvent to Apache Kafka for downstream analytics and notification services. An engineer writes:
@Transactional
public void confirmBooking(Booking booking) {
    bookingRepository.save(booking);
    kafkaTemplate.send("booking-events", new BookingConfirmedEvent(booking.getId()));
}
What dual-write inconsistency can occur in this implementation, and how does the Transactional Outbox pattern solve it?
